+From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: bornaradusin <bornaradusin@users.noreply.github.com>
+Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 23:30:00 +0000
+Subject: [PATCH] libxess, libxess_fg, libxell: stub Intel XeSS libraries for
+ games that ship them.
+
+Games that support Intel's XeSS upscaling ship Intel's libraries
+(libxess.dll for super resolution, libxess_fg.dll for frame generation,
+libxell.dll for latency): the game's PE import table references them
+directly, so they must exist for the loader to start the program.
+
+On the PS5's AMD GPU these libraries have no Intel device to talk to.
+The game's own copies initialise, get garbage back (no Intel GPU driver
+answers), and crash or return values the game takes for valid (EA Sports
+College Football 27). Disabling them with =d doesn't work because they
+are static imports: the loader refuses to start the program at all.
+
+These builtins export every public function from Intel's XeSS SDK 2.x
+headers but return XESS_RESULT_ERROR_UNSUPPORTED_DEVICE (-1) or
+XEFG_SWAPCHAIN_RESULT_ERROR_UNSUPPORTED_DEVICE (-1) from any call that
+creates a context or initialises: the game sees "no XeSS hardware" and
+falls back to its other upscaler (FSR, native). Functions that query
+version return 0, and destroy/cleanup functions succeed silently.
